<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jayavarman VII</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1181">1181</a>–<a href="/years/1218">1218</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jayavarman VII was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> king of the late twelfth and early thirteenth century. He drove out <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Cham</a> invaders who had sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a> and expanded the empire to its greatest extent. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana</a> Buddhist, he built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor-thom">Angkor Thom</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayon">Bayon</a> temple, hospitals and rest houses, and is regarded as one of the greatest rulers of the Khmer Empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jayavarman-vii.jpg" alt="Jayavarman VII" width="308" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Suzan Black · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:JayavarmanVII.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Jayavarman VII, whose name means victory armour and who is known posthumously as Mahaparamasaugata, was the son of King Dharanindravarman II and Queen Sri Jayarajacudamani. He was the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> king devoted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>; only one other had been a Buddhist. Suryavarman II, builder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor-wat">Angkor Wat</a>, died in 1150 and Dharanindravarman II ruled until 1160. Yashovarman II followed, but was overthrown by Tribhuvanadityavarman, assumed to be a usurper. In 1177, according to the usual account, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Cham</a> king Jaya Indravarman IV sailed a fleet up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong</a>, across Lake Tonlé Sap and up the Siem Reap River, sacked the capital Yasodharapura and had Tribhuvanadityavarman put to death. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-vickery">Michael Vickery</a> questions this date and the event, doubting the Chinese sources for the period.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Chams</a> occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> for four years. In his mid-50s Jayavarman led a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> army that drove them out, a naval battle shown on the walls of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayon">Bayon</a> and Banteay Chmar. He found the capital in disorder, ended the disputes between factions and was crowned in 1181. Early in his reign he probably repelled another Cham attack and put down a rebellion by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> kingdom of Malyang. Helped by the refugee prince Sri Vidyanandana, he sacked and conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champa">Champa</a> in 1190 and 1191, which remained a dependency for thirty years. He extended Khmer control up the Mekong Valley to Vientiane and south down the Kra Isthmus.</p><p>His building programme included 102 hospitals, 121 rest houses at intervals of 15 km along raised highways, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reservoir">reservoirs</a>. He then built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ta-prohm">Ta Prohm</a> for his mother, dedicated in 1186, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/preah-khan">Preah Khan</a> for his father, and finally the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayon">Bayon</a>, set in the new city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor-thom">Angkor Thom</a>, with the smaller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neak-pean">Neak Pean</a>. An inscription says he suffered the pain of his subjects more than his own. The works required the labour of thousands, and his reign saw the state centralised and people gathered into ever larger centres. He set up Buddha images in 23 towns of his empire.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Jayavarman died around 1218. Although he had many sons, only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indravarman-ii">Indravarman II</a> succeeded him, and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayavarman-viii">Jayavarman VIII</a>, a Shivaite, defaced or removed his Buddhist works: most of the Buddha images in the niches around the city wall were taken out, and the Buddha statue of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayon">Bayon</a> and others in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor-thom">Angkor Thom</a> were converted into linga. Historians generally consider him the most powerful of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> monarchs, and a bust of him is a masterpiece of the National Museum.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jayavarman VII” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Indravarman I drove them off in 799; by the Khmer, who conquered northern Champa in 1145 and under <b>Jayavarman VII</b> in the 1190s made it a dependency for thirty years; by the Vietnamese in 982, 1044, 1069, 1073, 1446 and 1471; and by the Mongol Yuan in 1283–85.</blockquote><a href="/empire/champa">Champa · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Expansion reached its height under rulers such as Suryavarman II, who campaigned against Dai Viet and Champa, and <b>Jayavarman VII</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/khmer-empire">Khmer Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>As Khmer power weakened after the reign of <b>Jayavarman VII</b>, Tai-speaking groups who had settled in the river valleys began to assert themselves.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sukhothai-kingdom">Sukhothai Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Chams sacked Angkor in 1178, <b>Jayavarman VII</b> restored the empire, built a new capital at Angkor Thom with the Bayon as its state temple, and dedicated it to Buddhism, encouraged by his wife Indradevi, a devout Mahayana Buddhist.</blockquote><a href="/work/angkor-wat">Angkor Wat · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>At its head stood a king whom the cult of the devaraja, the “god-king”, identified with Shiva or Vishnu, or under <b>Jayavarman VII</b> (r. 1181–1219) with the bodhisattva Avalokiteshvara, and whose temples, Angkor Wat and the Bayon, celebrated his divine rule on earth.</blockquote><a href="/societies/khmer-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Khmer Empire</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/cambodia">Cambodia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor-thom">Angkor Thom</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/cambodia">Cambodia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=13.43,103.83&amp;year=1199" aria-label="Angkor on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Jayavarman VII"><image href="/maps/globes/g09000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6140 3862h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6140 3862h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=13.43,103.83&amp;year=1199">What was here in 1199? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
